1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,800
Jack: I've still got loads to do for our report on nutritional food labels.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:13,200
Alice: Me too. What did you learn from doing the project about your own shopping habits?

3
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:20,506
Jack: Well, I've always had to check labels for traces of peanuts in everything I eat because of my allergy.

4
00:00:20,506 --> 00:00:26,350
But beyond that I've never really been concerned enough to check how healthy a product is.

5
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:32,370
Alice: This project has actually taught me to read the labels much more carefully.

6
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:37,100
I tended to believe claims on packaging like 'low in fat'.

7
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:44,000
But I now realise that the 'healthy' yoghurt I've bought for years is full of sugar

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,450
and that it's actually quite high in calories.

9
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:49,320
Jack: Ready meals are the worst

10
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:54,429
comparing the labels on supermarket pizzas was a real eye-opener.

11
00:00:54,429 --> 00:01:00,100
Did you have any idea how many calories they contain? I was amazed.

12
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:04,557
Alice: Yes, because unless you read the label really carefully,

13
00:01:04,557 --> 00:01:10,350
you wouldn't know that the nutritional values given are for half a pizza.

14
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:15,600
Jack: When most people eat the whole pizza. Not exactly transparent is it?

15
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:22,000
Alice: Not at all. But I expect it won't stop you from buying pizza?

16
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,738
Jack: Probably not, no! I thought comparing the different labelling systems used by food manufacturers was interesting.

17
00:01:29,738 --> 00:01:35,200
I think the kind of labelling system used makes a big difference.

18
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:37,550
Alice: Which one did you prefer?

19
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:42,663
Jack: I liked the traditional daily value system best - the one

20
00:01:42,663 --> 00:01:50,100
which tells you what proportion of your required daily intake of each ingredient the product contains.

21
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:56,045
I'm not sure it's the easiest for people to use but at least you get the full story.

22
00:01:56,045 --> 00:01:58,700
I like to know all the ingredients in a product

23
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:02,650
not just how much fat, salt and sugar they contain.

24
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:10,150
Alice: But it's good supermarkets have been making an effort to provide reliable information for customers.

25
00:02:10,150 --> 00:02:17,350
Jack: Yes. There just needs to be more consistency between labelling systems used by different supermarkets,

26
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:20,050
in terms of portion sizes, etc.

27
00:02:20,050 --> 00:02:28,150
Alice: Mmm. The labels on the different brands of chicken flavour crisps were quite revealing too, weren't they?

28
00:02:28,150 --> 00:02:32,745
Jack: Yeah. I don't understand how they can get away

29
00:02:32,745 --> 00:02:37,800
with calling them chicken flavour when they only contain artificial additives.

30
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:45,100
Alice: I know. I'd at least have expected them to contain a small percentage of real chicken.

31
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:46,850
Jack: Absolutely.

32
00:02:46,850 --> 00:02:52,660
Alice: I think having nutritional food labeling has been a good idea, don't you?

33
00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:55,780
I think it will change people's behaviour and stop mothers

34
00:02:55,940 --> 00:02:59,300
in particular, buying the wrong things.

35
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:04,094
Jack: But didn't that study kind of prove the opposite?

36
00:03:04,094 --> 00:03:07,450
People didn't necessarily stop buying unhealthy products.

37
00:03:07,450 --> 00:03:13,195
Alice: They only said that might be the case. Those findings weren't that conclusive

38
00:03:13,195 --> 00:03:19,350
and it was quite a small-scale study. I think more research has to be done.

39
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:22,000
Jack: Yes, I think you're probably right.

40
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,500
Jack: What do you think of the traffic-light system?

41
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:30,508
Alice: I think supermarkets like the idea of having a colour-coded system -

42
00:03:30,508 --> 00:03:36,350
red, orange or green - for levels of fat, sugar and salt in a product.

43
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:40,820
Jack: But it's not been adopted universally. And not on all products

44
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:43,200
Why do you suppose that is?

45
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:45,682
Alice: Pressure from the food manufacturers.

46
00:03:45,682 --> 00:03:52,300
Hardly surprising that some of them are opposed to flagging up how unhealthy their products are.

47
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:57,150
Jack: I'd have thought it would have been compulsory. It seems ridiculous it isn't.

48
00:03:57,150 --> 00:04:04,805
Alice: I know. And what I couldn't get over is the fact that it was brought in without enough consultation -

49
00:04:04,805 --> 00:04:08,250
a lot of experts had deep reservations about it.

50
00:04:08,250 --> 00:04:10,480
Jack: That is a bit weird.

51
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:14,020
I suppose there's an argument for doing the research now

52
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:16,800
when consumers are familiar with this system.

53
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:18,850
Alice: Yeah, maybe.

54
00:04:18,850 --> 00:04:24,000
Jack: The participants in the survey were quite positive about the traffic-light system.

55
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:28,720
Alice: Mmm. But I don't think they targeted the right people

56
00:04:29,180 --> 00:04:30,882
They should have focused on people

57
00:04:30,882 --> 00:04:36,550
with low literacy levels because these labels are designed to be accessible to them.

58
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:41,770
Jack: Yeah. But it's good to get feedback from all socio-economic groups.

59
00:04:41,770 --> 00:04:45,250
And there wasn't much variation in their responses.

60
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:49,192
Alice: No. But if they hadn't interviewed participants face-to-face,

61
00:04:49,192 --> 00:04:56,200
they could have used a much bigger sample size. I wonder why they chose that method?

62
00:04:56,200 --> 00:05:02,500
Jack: Dunno. How were they selected? Did they volunteer or were they approached?

63
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:09,552
Alice: I think they volunteered. The thing that wasn't stated was how often they bought packaged food -

64
00:05:09,552 --> 00:05:13,700
all we know is how frequently they used the supermarket.
